'''''Scabiosa atropurpurea''''' ([[synonym (taxonomy)|syn.]] ''Sixalix atropurpurea''), the '''mourningbride''', '''mournful widow''', '''pincushion flower''', or '''sweet scabious''', is an [[ornamental plant]] of the genus ''[[Scabiosa]]'' in the family [[Caprifoliaceae]].<ref>{{PLANTS |id=SCAT |taxon=Scabiosa atropurpurea |accessdate=6 November 2015}}</ref> It is native to southern Europe.<ref name="BaldwinGoldman2012">{{cite book |author1=Bruce G. Baldwin |author2=Douglas Goldman |author3=David J. Keil |author4=Robert Patterson |author5=Thomas J. Rosatti |title=The Digital Jepson Manual: Vascular Plants of California |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=GLgbblUM73UC&pg=PT2139 |date=7 February 2012 |publisher=University of California Press |isbn=978-0-520-95289-8 |pages=2139–}}</ref>
[[File:Scabiosa atropurpurea MHNT.BOT.2011.3.30.jpg|thumb|''Scabiosa atropurpurea'' - [[MHNT]]]]

==Subtaxa==
The following subtaxa are accepted:<ref name="319717-1" />

*''Scabiosa atropurpurea'' subsp. ''atropurpurea''
*''Scabiosa atropurpurea'' var. ''grandiflora'' {{small|(Scop.) Fiori & Paol.}}
*''Scabiosa atropurpurea'' subsp. ''maritima'' {{small|(L.) Arcang.}}

==Description==
''Scabiosa atropurpurea'' has [[cauline]] leaves that are [[Glossary of leaf morphology|pinnately dissected]],<ref name="BaldwinGoldman2012"/> plants have red to purple flowers. It is native to southern Europe. It propagates by seeds which are dispersed by animals.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2017-10-15 |title=''Scabiosa atropurpurea'' profile |url=https://www.cal-ipc.org/plants/profile/scabiosa-atropurpurea-profile/ |access-date=2020-12-02 |website=California Invasive Plant Council |language=en-US}}</ref>

==Cultivation==
It prefers cooler climates; high temperatures and humidity negatively impact its growth. ''Scabiosa atropurpurea'' grows best in rich, alkaline soils high in organic matter with good drainage.<ref>{{Cite web |title=''Scabiosa atropurpurea'' (Pincushion Flower, Scabiosa) {{!}} North Carolina Extension Gardener Plant Toolbox |url=https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/scabiosa-atropurpurea |access-date=2020-12-02 |website=plants.ces.ncsu.edu}}</ref> It makes a good cut flower.   
''Scabiosa atropurpurea'' 'Beaujolais Bonnets' is a cultivar grown for its long blooming period and burgundy flowers.<ref>{{Cite web |title=''Scabiosa atropurpurea'' 'Beaujolais Bonnets' - Plant Finder |url=http://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?taxonid=269827&isprofile=1&gen=Scabiosa |access-date=2020-12-02 |website=www.missouribotanicalgarden.org}}</ref>

==Invasive species==
It has become an [[invasive species]] outside of its native range, including the US state of California where it has invaded grasslands.<ref name="BaldwinGoldman2012"/> It ranks as one of South Australia's most commonly recorded weeds.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sweet Scabious, Pincushions (''Scabiosa atropurpurea'') – Weeds of Melbourne |url=https://weedsofmelbourne.org/sweet-scabious-pincushions-scabiosa-atropurpurea |access-date=2020-12-02 |language=en-AU}}</ref>

